👉 Use:

Managed Identity (advanced production)


🚀 STEP 1: Create Azure credentials (Service Principal)

Velero needs permission to store backups in Azure.

🔥 Run this:
az ad sp create-for-rbac \
  --name "velero-sp" \
  --role "Contributor" \
  --scopes /subscriptions/$(az account show --query id -o tsv)

👉 Output will look like:

{
  "appId": "xxxxx",
  "password": "xxxxx",
  "tenant": "xxxxx"
}

⚠️ Save this — VERY IMPORTANT

🚀 STEP 2: Create credentials file
nano credentials-velero

Paste this:

AZURE_SUBSCRIPTION_ID=<your-subscription-id>
AZURE_TENANT_ID=<tenant-id>
AZURE_CLIENT_ID=<appId>
AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET=<password>
AZURE_RESOURCE_GROUP=<your-resource-group>
AZURE_CLOUD_NAME=AzurePublicCloud

👉 Get subscription ID:

az account show --query id -o tsv
🚀 STEP 3: Install Velero in AKS

Now run (replace values):

velero install \
  --provider azure \
  --plugins velero/velero-plugin-for-microsoft-azure:v1.8.0 \
  --bucket velero-backups \
  --secret-file ./credentials-velero \
  --backup-location-config resourceGroup=<your-resource-group>,storageAccount=<your-storage-account> \
  --snapshot-location-config resourceGroup=<your-resource-group> \
  --use-node-agent
🚀 STEP 4: Verify installation
kubectl get pods -n velero

👉 Expected:

velero-xxxxx   Running
🚀 STEP 5: Take your first backup

👉 Backup entire cluster:

velero backup create first-backup
🔍 Check backup
velero backup get

👉 Status should be:

Completed
🚀 STEP 6: Test restore (VERY IMPORTANT)
Create test namespace:
kubectl create ns test-velero
Delete it:
kubectl delete ns test-velero
Restore:
velero restore create --from-backup first-backup

👉 Namespace comes back = SUCCESS ✅

🔥 STEP 7: Schedule automatic backups
velero schedule create daily-backup \
  --schedule="0 2 * * *"

👉 Daily backup at 2 AM

